# What never belongs in a build context.
#
# THE FIRST TWO LINES ARE THE ONES THAT MATTER. `node_modules` is 1.1 GB of
# darwin-arm64 binaries that would be uploaded to the daemon on every build and
# then overwritten by the image's own linux-musl install; `.env` holds the
# Anthropic key, the Telegram token and the Firebase pair, and a build context
# is not a secret store — a file copied into a layer is in the image for anyone
# who pulls it, whatever a later `rm` does.
node_modules
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# The React client's GENERATED trees, and only those. Docker matches patterns
# against the full relative path, so the bare `node_modules` and `dist` above
# catch the ROOT ones and not these — which `COPY apps ./apps` would then send
# to the daemon and into the build stage on every build, a 200 MB darwin-arm64
# tree the image immediately overwrites with its own install.
#
# THE SOURCE STAYS IN THE CONTEXT DELIBERATELY: the client is built inside the
# image and served from the dashboard pod, so a blanket `apps/web` would copy
# nothing for that stage to build.
apps/web/node_modules
apps/web/dist

# Build output. The image compiles its own from source; copying a host build in
# would ship whatever was last on this laptop.
dist
coverage
*.tsbuildinfo

# Test and tooling residue.
test-results
playwright-report
data/corpus
*.log

# Version control and editor state.
.git
.gitignore
.idea
.vscode
.superpowers

# Docs and CI: nothing in the image reads them, and every byte here is a byte
# sent to the daemon on every build.
docs
README.md
CLAUDE.md
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml
Caddyfile
